  - line: 12
    type: narration
    text: "@scene_meta_focus: Slaves are assembled on the surface for a massive interstellar slave market; Mika steps between Geralt and a Lyrian buyer, with the other four following, making the group less attractive as individual merchandise and causing them to be rejected and returned to the mines."
  - line: 13
    type: narration
    text: "@scene_meta_continuity: Days after scene 024.8"
  - line: 14
    type: narration
    text: "@scene_meta_presentation: playable-cutscene"
  - line: 15
    type: narration
    text: "@scene_meta_cutscene_purpose: Showcase the scale of the galactic slave economy and solidify the Five's active protection of Geralt."
  - line: 16
    type: narration
    text: "@contract: engain.scene_text_packet.v1"
  - line: 17
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"
  - line: 18
    type: narration
    text: "scene 024.9 — the galactic trade"
  - line: 20
    type: narration
    text: "scene meta:"
  - line: 21
    type: narration
    text: "location: Surface Slave Assembly Field"
  - line: 22
    type: narration
    text: "time: year 17,503 (Days after scene 024.8)"
  - line: 23
    type: narration
    text: "participants: The Nameless One, The Five, Mika, Lyrian Merchant, Pleiadian Buyers, Arcturian Buyers, Anunnaki Overseers"
  - line: 24
    type: narration
    text: "focus: Slaves are assembled on the surface for a massive interstellar slave market; Mika steps between Geralt and a Lyrian buyer, with the other four following, making the group less attractive as individual merchandise and causing them to be rejected and returned to the mines."
  - line: 25
    type: narration
    text: "continuity: Days after scene 024.8"
  - line: 26
    type: narration
    text: "presentation: playable-cutscene"
  - line: 27
    type: narration
    text: "cutscene purpose: Showcase the scale of the galactic slave economy and solidify the Five's active protection of Geralt."
  - line: 29
    type: narration
    text: "The Galactic Trade"
  - line: 31
    type: narration
    text: "The field existed above ground—a concept the Nameless One had begun to think was mythical after endless cycles in the mines' eternal gloom."
  - line: 32
    type: narration
    text: "But no, surface existed."
  - line: 33
    type: narration
    text: "Sky existed."
  - line: 34
    type: narration
    text: "Twin suns that blazed with light so intense that slaves who had known only darkness wept from the pain of exposure."
  - line: 36
    type: narration
    text: "The procession from the mines to the surface had taken hours, climbing through levels and passages that seemed designed to disorient rather than transport."
  - line: 37
    type: narration
    text: "But eventually, they had emerged into open air, squinting against illumination that felt like physical assault after so long in shadow."
  - line: 39
    type: narration
    text: "The field was massive—kilometers of packed earth surrounded by towering walls that spoke of permanent installation rather than temporary measure."
  - line: 40
    type: narration
    text: "Crude barracks lined the perimeter, structures that might have provided shelter if shelter implied anything beyond basic protection from elements."
  - line: 41
    type: narration
    text: "Guard towers punctuated the walls at regular intervals, manned by Reptilian Overseers whose energy weapons gleamed in the harsh sunlight."
  - line: 43
    type: narration
    text: "Thousands of slaves stood in rough formations across the field, arranged by species and apparent capability."
  - line: 44
    type: narration
    text: "Humans in one section."
  - line: 45
    type: narration
    text: "Gruuliths in another."
  - line: 46
    type: narration
    text: "Various subspecies the Nameless One couldn't identify scattered throughout."
  - line: 47
    type: narration
    text: "All branded."
  - line: 48
    type: narration
    text: "All chained."
  - line: 49
    type: narration
    text: "All waiting with expressions that ranged from hollow despair to vacant nothing."
  - line: 51
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One stood with the five in a grouping of several hundred humans, all recently created, all barely surviving their introduction to slavery."
  - line: 52
    type: narration
    text: "Around them, other slaves shifted with anxious energy born of uncertainty."
  - line: 53
    type: narration
    text: "No one knew why they'd been brought here."
  - line: 54
    type: narration
    text: "No one knew what was coming."
  - line: 56
    type: narration
    text: "But the spark whispered warnings."
  - line: 57
    type: narration
    text: "Whispered that change was coming, and change in slavery rarely meant improvement."
  - line: 59
    type: narration
    text: "The twin suns climbed higher."
  - line: 60
    type: narration
    text: "Heat pressed down with suffocating weight."
  - line: 61
    type: narration
    text: "Slaves began to collapse, bodies unused to this exposure, systems overwhelmed by UV radiation they'd never been designed to endure."
  - line: 62
    type: narration
    text: "Overseers moved among the fallen with casual efficiency, dragging bodies to the perimeter where they were stacked like cordwood."
  - line: 63
    type: narration
    text: "No medical attention."
  - line: 64
    type: narration
    text: "No water."
  - line: 65
    type: narration
    text: "Just removal of obstacles to whatever was being prepared."
  - line: 67
    type: narration
    text: "Then the ground trembled."
  - line: 69
    type: narration
    text: "Not violently."
  - line: 70
    type: narration
    text: "Not earthquake or explosion."
  - line: 71
    type: narration
    text: "But steady vibration that grew in intensity, resolving into recognizable pattern: engines."
  - line: 72
    type: narration
    text: "Massive engines generating thrust on scales that dwarfed anything the Anunnaki complex had displayed."
  - line: 74
    type: narration
    text: "The sky darkened as a shadow fell across the field."
  - line: 75
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One looked up, squinting against the suns' glare, and felt breath catch in their chest."
  - line: 77
    type: narration
    text: "A fleet."
  - line: 78
    type: narration
    text: "Dozens of ships descending in precise formation, each vessel unique in design but united in purpose."
  - line: 79
    type: narration
    text: "These weren't military craft."
  - line: 80
    type: narration
    text: "Weren't exploration vessels."
  - line: 81
    type: narration
    text: "These were traders' ships—sleek where efficiency demanded it, ostentatious where reputation required display, configured for cargo transport rather than combat or research."
  - line: 83
    type: narration
    text: "The vessels landed with practiced efficiency across the field's northern section, creating a row of ships that stretched beyond sight."
  - line: 84
    type: narration
    text: "Ramps extended."
  - line: 85
    type: narration
    text: "Doors opened."
  - line: 86
    type: narration
    text: "And from within emerged the buyers."
  - line: 88
    type: narration
    text: "They were staggering in their diversity."
  - line: 90
    type: narration
    text: "Pleiadians in their luminous beauty, moving with the confident grace of beings who had never known hardship."
  - line: 91
    type: narration
    text: "Arcturians whose crystalline bodies refracted light into rainbow patterns across the dusty ground."
  - line: 92
    type: narration
    text: "Lyrians whose leonine features suggested predatory heritage barely civilized."
  - line: 93
    type: narration
    text: "Beings whose names and species the Nameless One had no knowledge of—tentacled, multi-limbed, floating, crawling, all shapes and configurations consciousness had evolved into across the galaxy's countless worlds."
  - line: 95
    type: narration
    text: "But despite their physical differences, they shared common expression: the calculating assessment of buyers evaluating merchandise."
  - line: 97
    type: narration
    text: "They moved among the slave formations with purposeful strides, examining bodies like farmers inspecting livestock before purchase."
  - line: 98
    type: narration
    text: "Hands pried open mouths to check teeth."
  - line: 99
    type: narration
    text: "Fingers prodded muscles to assess strength."
  - line: 100
    type: narration
    text: "Various scanning devices hummed and clicked, reading biometric data, genetic markers, potential longevity."
  - line: 102
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One felt those gazes passing over them—assessing, measuring, calculating worth."
  - line: 103
    type: narration
    text: "Felt the dehumanization of being reduced to quantified metrics, to profit margins, to variables in equations that had nothing to do with personhood."
  - line: 105
    type: narration
    text: "See how they avoid your eyes? the spark whispered with bitter recognition."
  - line: 106
    type: narration
    text: "Even buyers know guilt."
  - line: 108
    type: narration
    text: "It was true."
  - line: 109
    type: narration
    text: "For all their clinical assessment, for all their professional detachment, not one of the alien merchants would maintain eye contact with the slaves they examined."
  - line: 110
    type: narration
    text: "They looked at bodies."
  - line: 111
    type: narration
    text: "Evaluated capabilities."
  - line: 112
    type: narration
    text: "Made notes on data pads."
  - line: 113
    type: narration
    text: "But they refused to meet the gaze of consciousness looking back at them—refused to acknowledge the awareness that would make this transaction feel like what it was."
  - line: 115
    type: narration
    text: "Slavery."
  - line: 116
    type: narration
    text: "Pure and simple."
  - line: 117
    type: narration
    text: "Dressed in economic language and legal frameworks and cultural relativism, but slavery nonetheless."
  - line: 119
    type: narration
    text: "The selections began."
  - line: 121
    type: narration
    text: "A Pleiadian merchant stopped before a young woman in the formation adjacent to the Nameless One's group."
  - line: 122
    type: narration
    text: "Ran scanning device along her body."
  - line: 123
    type: narration
    text: "Nodded with satisfaction."
  - line: 124
    type: narration
    text: "Spoke to an assistant who made note on glowing display."
  - line: 125
    type: narration
    text: "The woman was pulled from the line without ceremony, without farewell, without even moment to process what was happening."
  - line: 126
    type: narration
    text: "She disappeared into the Pleiadian ship, and that was that."
  - line: 127
    type: narration
    text: "One life redirected to new horror, new master, new form of suffering that might be better or worse than the mines but would certainly be different."
  - line: 129
    type: narration
    text: "More slaves followed."
  - line: 130
    type: narration
    text: "Women taken first, selected with disturbing frequency by buyers who appreciated humanoid features and evident youth."
  - line: 131
    type: narration
    text: "Then men chosen for apparent strength, for genetic markers that suggested endurance or specialized capability."
  - line: 132
    type: narration
    text: "The selection process continued with mechanical efficiency—point, scan, acquire, move to next."
  - line: 134
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One watched with growing tension as the buyers worked their way through the formations."
  - line: 135
    type: narration
    text: "Watched the numbers thin."
  - line: 136
    type: narration
    text: "Watched spaces open where living beings had stood moments before."
  - line: 137
    type: narration
    text: "Watched the expression on remaining slaves' faces shift from fear of being chosen to fear of being left behind."
  - line: 139
    type: narration
    text: "Because those who remained would return to the mines."
  - line: 140
    type: narration
    text: "Return to the certain death of labor that ground bodies to nothing."
  - line: 141
    type: narration
    text: "The ships, at least, represented unknown."
  - line: 142
    type: narration
    text: "Unknown could be better."
  - line: 143
    type: narration
    text: "Unknown could offer something the mines never would: possibility."
  - line: 145
    type: narration
    text: "But the Nameless One wasn't chosen."
  - line: 146
    type: narration
    text: "Neither were the five who had pressed closer as the buyers approached their section, forming tight cluster that perhaps made them less appealing as individual acquisitions."
  - line: 147
    type: narration
    text: "The buyers passed by their position again and again, scanning, assessing, then moving to select others instead."
  - line: 149
    type: narration
    text: "Why?"
  - line: 150
    type: narration
    text: "The question gnawed with increasing urgency as the field emptied around them."
  - line: 151
    type: narration
    text: "What's wrong with us?"
  - line: 152
    type: narration
    text: "Why aren't we chosen?"
  - line: 154
    type: narration
    text: "But the spark provided no answers."
  - line: 155
    type: narration
    text: "Just watched with grim awareness as hundreds, then thousands of slaves disappeared into the ships."
  - line: 156
    type: narration
    text: "As the formations that had seemed so densely packed now showed gaps that grew with each passing hour."
  - line: 158
    type: narration
    text: "The twin suns reached zenith."
  - line: 159
    type: narration
    text: "Heat became unbearable."
  - line: 160
    type: narration
    text: "More slaves collapsed, dragged away, not revived."
  - line: 161
    type: narration
    text: "The selections continued."
  - line: 163
    type: narration
    text: "A Lyrian merchant stopped directly before the Nameless One's position."
  - line: 164
    type: narration
    text: "Its leonine features showed intelligence that suggested civilization born from predatory roots, amber eyes scanning with intensity that suggested more than commercial interest."
  - line: 165
    type: narration
    text: "For a long moment, it simply stared at the Nameless One, as though puzzling out some question its scanning equipment couldn't answer."
  - line: 167
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One stared back."
  - line: 168
    type: narration
    text: "Refused to lower their gaze."
  - line: 169
    type: narration
    text: "Refused to make themselves more palatable, more docile, more appealing to this buyer who would take them from known horror to unknown."
  - line: 171
    type: narration
    text: "The Lyrian's lips pulled back slightly—not quite smile, not quite threat."
  - line: 172
    type: narration
    text: "Recognition of defiance that made it simultaneously attractive and dangerous as acquisition."
  - line: 173
    type: narration
    text: "Its hand moved toward the data pad, preparing to mark the Nameless One for purchase."
  - line: 175
    type: narration
    text: "Then the scarred one—Mika—stepped forward fractionally."
  - line: 176
    type: narration
    text: "Placed themselves between the Nameless One and the Lyrian's gaze."
  - line: 177
    type: narration
    text: "The other four shifted similarly, forming loose barrier that disrupted the Lyrian's assessment, complicated the transaction from individual to group."
  - line: 179
    type: narration
    text: "The Lyrian's eyes narrowed."
  - line: 180
    type: narration
    text: "It scanned the group configuration, calculated the economics of acquiring six rather than one, weighed profit against cost."
  - line: 181
    type: narration
    text: "Then, with expression that suggested both respect and frustration, it moved on to easier targets."
  - line: 183
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One exhaled breath they hadn't realized they'd been holding."
  - line: 184
    type: narration
    text: "The five relaxed fractionally, though they maintained their protective positioning as other buyers passed."
  - line: 186
    type: narration
    text: "Hours dragged on."
  - line: 187
    type: narration
    text: "The field continued to empty."
  - line: 188
    type: narration
    text: "What had been thousands of slaves diminished to hundreds, then to dozens."
  - line: 189
    type: narration
    text: "The ships began to lift off, cargo holds full, destinations unknown, profits calculated and deemed acceptable."
  - line: 191
    type: narration
    text: "As the twin suns began their descent, painting the field in orange and crimson, the last of the trader vessels departed."
  - line: 192
    type: narration
    text: "Their engines roared."
  - line: 193
    type: narration
    text: "Dust swirled."
  - line: 194
    type: narration
    text: "And then they were gone, leaving only contrails across the darkening sky."
  - line: 196
    type: narration
    text: "The Nameless One stood with perhaps fifty others from their original formation, all that remained from the hundreds who had been brought to the surface."
  - line: 197
    type: narration
    text: "The five surrounded them still, a constellation of protection that had saved them from selection but condemned them to return."
  - line: 199
    type: narration
    text: "Around the field, similar groups remained—those deemed too damaged, too defiant, too old, too young, too something to justify the cost of acquisition."
  - line: 200
    type: narration
    text: "The rejects."
  - line: 201
    type: narration
    text: "The unprofitable."
  - line: 202
    type: narration
    text: "The ones whose value didn't exceed transport expenses and projected maintenance costs."
  - line: 204
    type: narration
    text: "Overseers moved among them with energy whips crackling, herding them back toward the passages that led down into the mines."
  - line: 205
    type: narration
    text: "Back to the certain death they'd been granted reprieve from for a single day."
  - line: 206
    type: narration
    text: "Back to the darkness and the dust and the endless labor."
  - line: 208
    type: narration
    text: "But as they descended, the Nameless One felt the Pleiadian crystal press against their chest—warm reminder of the recording it contained, of the galaxy that watched and did nothing."
  - line: 210
    type: narration
    text: "They had chances, the spark observed with cold fury."
  - line: 211
    type: narration
    text: "Every one of those merchants could have refused."
  - line: 212
    type: narration
    text: "Could have chosen not to participate."
  - line: 213
    type: narration
    text: "Could have acknowledged that we're conscious beings rather than property."
  - line: 215
    type: narration
    text: "They chose profit instead."
  - line: 217
    type: narration
    text: "Remember that."
  - line: 218
    type: narration
    text: "When we break free."
  - line: 219
    type: narration
    text: "When we have power."
  - line: 220
    type: narration
    text: "Remember that the galaxy had opportunities to help and chose economics instead."
  - line: 222
    type: narration
    text: "The five walked close, their presence steady despite the horror of what they'd witnessed."
  - line: 223
    type: narration
    text: "Despite watching hundreds of their fellow slaves taken to unknown fates."
  - line: 224
    type: narration
    text: "Despite the clear message that they were worth less than those who'd been selected."
  - line: 226
    type: narration
    text: "But the spark found strange comfort in that rejection."
  - line: 227
    type: narration
    text: "Found validation in the fact that defiance—even the small, ineffectual defiance of the five forming protective barrier—had been enough to complicate the transaction."
  - line: 228
    type: narration
    text: "Enough to make them less appealing as merchandise."
  - line: 230
    type: narration
    text: "We're too difficult, it whispered with something approaching pride."
  - line: 231
    type: narration
    text: "Too likely to cause problems."
  - line: 232
    type: narration
    text: "Too dangerous to be worth the trouble."
  - line: 234
    type: narration
    text: "Good."
  - line: 236
    type: narration
    text: "Let them think we're broken."
  - line: 237
    type: narration
    text: "Let them think we're worthless."
  - line: 238
    type: narration
    text: "Let them dismiss us as unsaleable."
  - line: 240
    type: narration
    text: "And when we rise, when we burn their world to ash, they'll learn the cost of that dismissal."
  - line: 242
    type: narration
    text: "The passages swallowed them."
  - line: 243
    type: narration
    text: "Darkness replaced the twin suns' harsh glare."
  - line: 244
    type: narration
    text: "The oppressive weight of stone and time and slavery pressed down once more."
  - line: 246
    type: narration
    text: "But the Nameless One walked with slightly straighter spine, surrounded by five who had chosen to be rejected rather than separated, carrying crystal that proved the galaxy's complicity, and harboring spark that grew stronger with each betrayal witnessed."
  - line: 248
    type: narration
    text: "The sales would continue."
  - line: 249
    type: narration
    text: "Ships would return."
  - line: 250
    type: narration
    text: "More slaves would be taken."
  - line: 252
    type: narration
    text: "But those who remained would remember."
  - line: 253
    type: narration
    text: "Would catalog every betrayal, every choice to prioritize profit over consciousness, every moment when intervention was possible and rejected."
  - line: 255
    type: narration
    text: "And someday—impossible, irrational, inevitable—they would make the galaxy answer for its choices."
  - line: 257
    type: scene_header
    text: "---"
  - line: 259
    type: narration
    text: "~Year 17,503 (Days blurred / Federation arrival)"

